Test an archive's integrity: read every entry to the end, checking CRCs and codec checksums.
Fails with `Corrupt`, listing the damaged entries in `details`.

Usage: beezip test [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE>

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

Options:
  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password for encrypted entries

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How names stored as bytes are read: zip names without the UTF-8 flag, tar names outside
          pax records, cpio, ar, LHA, ARJ, ACE, ZOO, ARC and squashfs names, CAB names without the
          UTF-8 flag, ISO names outside Joliet [default: from config, `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

      --recover[=<RECOVER>]
          Open a .bhive whose index is missing or damaged (a download cut short) from the records
          before its cells, with the entries they still hold
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --require-signer <SIGNER>
          Refuse the archive unless it is signed, validly, by each of these: a `bzsig1…` key, a
          certificate file, or the name of a signing key or contact. Matched by their whole SHA-256,
          never a fingerprint (docs/M14.md §11)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -i, --identity <FILE>
          A key file that opens encrypted .bhive archives (age identities, PEM and PKCS #8 keys);
          repeatable

      --password-stdin
          Read the password or recovery key from the first line of stdin, where other programs can't
          see it as they can the command line

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
